        "word_count": 180,
        "duration_sec": null,
        "body_text": "Pseudoruminococcus massiliensis is a species of bacterium, which was first isolated from the gut microbiota of a healthy Senegalese faecal transplant donor. The bacterium was named after the French city of Marseille, where the strain was isolated in 2017. == Structure == P. massiliensis cells are cocci: they are spherical/round-shaped. The bacterium’s cells are 0.6x45 μm in size and non-motile (it is incapable of moving independently). P. massiliensis colonies are grey, smooth and transparent. Colonies are, on average, 0.2-1.5mm in diameter. When grown on a specific medium (GAM agar), the bacterium has been observed to form protrusions, which could be identified as pili or fimbriae: these appendages on the surface of the bacterium e.g. helps it attach to surfaces. Scientists suggest these protrusions could play a role in the successful colonization of P. massiliensis. The bacterium, however, does not adhere well to mucus and might therefore not be able to stick to the epithelium of the colon. == Phylogeny == The closest related species to P. massiliensis is Ruminococcus bromii. R. bromii is considered a keystone species within the human gut ecosystem. == References ==",
        "audio_url": null,
        "description": null,
